// RED TEAM round 3, concurrency lens: does an uncaught `throw` inside a
// SPAWNED thread's task function permanently hang everyone else waiting on
// that thread's WaitGroup signal?
//
// Read (not guessed) `kryos-rt/src/spawn.rs::kryos_spawn`: the spawned OS
// thread's closure runs `invoke_task(fn_ptr, &args)` then calls
// `kryos_exception_report_thread_if_pending()` -- the comment says exactly
// "A `throw` that unwound out of the thread's entry function would otherwise
// vanish with the thread-local state. Report it like a Rust thread panic:
// message to stderr, thread dies, process lives." So an uncaught `throw`
// inside a spawned task's own top-level function is designed to kill ONLY
// that thread and let the process continue -- correct and documented-in-code
// behavior, matching Rust's own thread::spawn + panic semantics.
//
// The hazard this program probes is a CONSEQUENCE the design does not call
// out anywhere: whatever Kryos statements were supposed to run AFTER the
// throw point inside that same spawned function body simply never execute,
// because "thread dies" means the whole function body stops at the throw --
// there is no unwind-then-continue, no finally, and the exception-check
// early-return path only propagates the exception UP through the current
// function's own call stack, it does not resume after. If the killed
// function's job was to call `wg_done(wg)` once it finished (the ordinary
// "worker signals completion" idiom shown in std::chan's own doc examples
// and in this campaign's own attack_actor_multiword_send_contention.kry /
// attack_spawn_mutating_closure_reentrancy.kry files), that wg_done() is
// silently skipped -- and `wg_wait()` in main spins on
// `while self.counter.load() > 0 { sleep(1) }` (std::sync::WaitGroup.wait)
// with NOTHING that will ever decrement the counter back to the target.
// That's not a race or a corruption -- it's a deterministic, 100%-reproducible
// permanent hang triggered by exactly ONE ordinary uncaught exception among
// many workers, with no diagnostic pointing at the real cause (the spawned
// thread DOES print "kryos: uncaught exception in spawned thread: ..." to
// stderr, but nothing connects that message to the main thread silently
// hanging forever afterward).
use std::chan::{new_wait_group, wg_add, wg_done, wg_wait}

fn risky_work(n: i64) -> i64 {
    if n == 3 {
        throw "boom in worker " + to_string(n)
    }
    return n * 2
}

fn main() {
    let mut wg = new_wait_group()
    let mut i = 0
    while i < 8 {
        wg = wg_add(wg, 1)
        let idx = i
        spawn {
            // ordinary worker idiom: do work, then signal done -- no
            // try/catch, matching how most of this campaign's own spawn
            // repros are written (they don't guard against a throw either)
            let r = risky_work(idx)
            println("worker " + to_string(idx) + " result=" + to_string(r))
            wg_done(wg)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    println("main: waiting on wg...")
    wg_wait(wg)
    println("main: all workers done (this line should be unreachable if the hang hypothesis is correct)")
}
